WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.804 --> 00:00:08.400
Chris: We had presented to OpenAI, to a group&nbsp;
of researchers. I remember I was presenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.200
and half the audience was just looking at&nbsp;
their laptops. I left that meeting feeling like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.840
no one cares. Then a week later,&nbsp;
Woj calls us up and he says, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.080
we got this problem on the robotics team.&nbsp;
Come check this out. Can you help us out?"&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.400
We go, look, and I remember going there with&nbsp;
Shawn. Both Shawn and I are looking at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.520
and we're excited, right? Because we can&nbsp;
solve the problem. They're telling us they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.360
have a problem and they want us to fix it.
Lukas: You're listening to Gradient Dissent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.680
a show about machine learning in the&nbsp;
real world. I'm your host, Lukas Biewald.&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:54.400
This episode is a fun departure from our normal&nbsp;
format, where I interview two people in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.560
machine learning space about their work. But&nbsp;
these two people happen to be my co-founders of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.840
Weights &amp; Biases. Shawn Lewis, our CTO, and&nbsp;
Chris Van Pelt, our Corporate Vice-President.&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.880
I talk to them about questions that have been&nbsp;
stewing inside of me for years, like "Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.880
did we start this company?" and "Where is it&nbsp;
going?" Honestly, I was surprised and educated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.560
by their answers and I hope that you enjoy&nbsp;
listening to this episode as much as I enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.080
asking them these hard questions.
Lukas: All right. Here we go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.960
Should we just jump right in?
How did this company start? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.120
ask this because it's the most common&nbsp;
question I get asked if I go on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.200
other podcast or with any candidate. Everyone&nbsp;
wants to know how did you start the company.&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.040
I was kind of realizing you two both have&nbsp;
founding stories that's telling the same story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:57.840
but it may have diverged in its evolution. I&nbsp;
would be curious to hear you two's version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.160
the story. Which of you wants to go first?
Chris: Shawn, I think you should go first.&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.440
Shawn: All right. When I started my career&nbsp;
as a software engineer, I was at Google and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.320
was in the platforms team at Google.&nbsp;
I joined...this was back in 2006. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.480
worked on all kinds of stuff in the platforms&nbsp;
team. The platforms team was responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.880
building the data centers and all the machines&nbsp;
inside the data centers that Google uses.&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.720
I wrote a lot of the software that ran in those&nbsp;
environments. When you write tests like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.200
they generate a ton of data. At first, I was&nbsp;
writing tests, but then I ended up with just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.400
all of this data. Really, where I ended up&nbsp;
spending my time was on making tools and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:47.040
data pipelines that would help us understand that&nbsp;
data. That's what I realized that I loved doing.&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.840
I ended up writing all kinds of different tools&nbsp;
and data pipelines — and the data's in all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.360
different databases all over Google — and I merged&nbsp;
it into once place, and defined the metrics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.160
we used to understand hard drives. And then made&nbsp;
these user-facing tools — and when I say user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.120
I mean other people at Google — that they&nbsp;
could use to dig in and understand this data.&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.240
I worked on a lot of stuff like that at Google.&nbsp;
It was super fun. Then I eventually left.&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.093
Then I started this other company called Beep.&nbsp;
We built hardware. We built a lot of things. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.093 --> 00:03:20.960
is kind of tangential to the story so I won't&nbsp;
go into it, but we were also in Y Combinator.&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:28.240
In that Y Combinator batch was actually Lukas's&nbsp;
wife, Noga, who I'm not sure if folks know this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.640
but she's the founder of a company called&nbsp;
PicnicHealth. We were in a Y Combinator batch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.760
we ended up sharing an office together in the&nbsp;
Mission in San Francisco for a couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.840
and I got to know Lukas that way.
We had a hardware component at Beep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.680
We had this hardware lab. Lukas loves robots&nbsp;
and he was kind of always tinkering in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.960
garage building robots. He would come up to my&nbsp;
hardware lab and poke his head through the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.960
and go, "What are you guys doing in here? Also,&nbsp;
my robot's broken," and we'd just start to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.040
to know each other and work on the robot and help&nbsp;
him fix it. So we became good friends that way.&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.040
As Beep wound down — I don't&nbsp;
know, I think that was 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.640
I think, this is my version of&nbsp;
the story — Chris and Lukas were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.600
starting to step out of Figure Eight around&nbsp;
the same time. The circumstances were really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.880
good. Deep learning was starting to take&nbsp;
off. We were all really excited about it.&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.760
I had been thinking about these problems a lot. I&nbsp;
loved the chance to work with Lukas and Chris, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.880
we just started hanging out, and talking&nbsp;
about it, and jumped on that opportunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.160
and started building the stuff that&nbsp;
I needed to organize that data.&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.480
It was an exciting time and it still is.
Lukas: All right. Chris, I think you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.200
a different version of the story. Let's hear it.
Chris: All right. We got to go back. We got to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:51.600
back to like 2006, like Shawn. Except in&nbsp;
my 2006, I'm coming to San Francisco for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.920
the first time and it's to work at an exciting&nbsp;
startup. I feel like I've made it. I've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.120
doing web development and trying to advance my&nbsp;
career and happened to be into Ruby on Rails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:12.560
which was really hot and exciting at the time.
There was this startup in San Francisco that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:20.720
using Ruby on Rails and using machine learning to&nbsp;
create a smarter, hopefully more relevant search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.760
engine. That startup was Powerset and I came up&nbsp;
to San Francisco — it was like the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.320
2007 — and that's actually where I met Lukas.
Lukas and I joined Powerset at roughly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.800
same time. I was in the product team interfacing&nbsp;
with a whole bunch of the other backend teams to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:45.200
try to create an interface to this exciting&nbsp;
new tool. Fast forward about a year or so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:51.520
and Luke and I decided, "Powerset has been fun,&nbsp;
but I think it's time for us to give our own go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.320
at creating a Powerset or a startup."
We set out to make crowdsourcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.920
more accessible to the enterprise and people&nbsp;
that wanted to do it to collect training data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:08.240
to train machine learning models. Way before&nbsp;
machine learning was as cool as it is today.&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:15.360
Yeah, fast forward about 10 years through&nbsp;
creating a startup with Lukas, learning a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:22.880
along the way. At the time that we started&nbsp;
Weights &amp; Biases, Luke and I's day-to-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:30.160
responsibilities at the company were winding&nbsp;
down. We were asking ourselves, "What is next?"&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:37.680
I remember going to Lukas's workshop, where most&nbsp;
of his podcasts are recorded and playing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:44.000
robots. I was really into Lua for a little while&nbsp;
there, thinking we could make a cool Lua toolkit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:54.080
for robots. But then it was really Lukas having an&nbsp;
internship at OpenAI and actually building models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.520
with some of the world's most&nbsp;
renowned machine learning researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:07.280
and needing tools to help him get his job done&nbsp;
that was the initial itch that we were scratching.&nbsp;

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.880
I love building tools. Luke's like, "Hey, I need&nbsp;
a tool to help me build models at OpenAI." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.080
"Great. Let me try to whip something up,"&nbsp;
and just really poured myself into making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.120
a very early prototype. Then shortly&nbsp;
thereafter, Shawn came into the picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.360
and I am forever grateful.
Lukas: Awesome, man. I was getting some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.560
messages from Lavanya to pull you both back on&nbsp;
the rails while you were talking, but I love the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.600
extended cut founding stories. I think mine is&nbsp;
like a sentence or two. Sorry, Lavanya. Now she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.840
texting me what the... But, no secrets here.
Lukas: Actually, this is a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.760
segue into another question for you.
Shawn, you've been out on paternity leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.680
Chris, you've been talking to lots&nbsp;
of customers independently from me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.000
People keep asking me this, they're like, "What is&nbsp;
the architecture of the Weights &amp; Biases server?"&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:06.960
I try to describe it and I realize I honestly&nbsp;
have no idea. I know there's MySQL involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:12.560
and React. Can you give me the several&nbsp;
sentence lay of the land? Say I'm an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.000
engineering candidate and I just want to know&nbsp;
what are we using, how does it all fit together.&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.080
Chris: Okay. All right.
We got a single page React&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:25.520
application that is our front end. It's just a lot&nbsp;
of JavaScript. We load that up into the browser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.880
and then it makes requests against our GraphQL&nbsp;
backend, which happens to be written in Golang.&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:38.080
When a customer wants to run Weights &amp; Biases&nbsp;
themselves, we actually deliver all of this — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.360
single-page React app and the GraphQL backend API&nbsp;
— in a single Docker container that they could run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:50.640
within their Kubernetes cluster or in a managed&nbsp;
Terraform-based deployment that we support. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:58.480
the backend persistent stores are super simple.&nbsp;
We've got MySQL and an S3-Compatible Object Store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.080
or Azure Blob storage or Google Cloud Storage.
There's a little Redis in there, but customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.440
generally don't have to worry about that.
Shawn: It's nice because from early on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.960
we knew that having the potential to go on-prem&nbsp;
was really important for our customers because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.360
of data privacy concerns, because these&nbsp;
datasets are so valuable and have all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.600
other privacy concerns. We really just&nbsp;
kept it simple to make that possible and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:30.240
yeah, those were good early choices.
Lukas: Shawn, you wrote a document at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:36.720
one point that was, "What it would take to be a&nbsp;
billion-dollar business?" for Weights &amp; Biases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.040
I thought maybe we could pull it up and&nbsp;
compare it to what it actually took to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.600
be a billion-dollar market cap business.
Looking at this document, how do you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.800
that things have played out? Has anything&nbsp;
played out differently than you expected?&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:00.400
Shawn: The core of the argument was, "We're&nbsp;
not sure if we can build better products than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.600
everybody else in the space, but we can raise&nbsp;
a lot of money." We know we can do that and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:12.720
are well connected to lots of customers because of&nbsp;
Lukas and Chris's background with Figure Eight. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:18.320
it was very easy for us in the early days to&nbsp;
go into a customer with very little to show.&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.240
We had a demo of the early parts of the product&nbsp;
and have good conversations. That's really what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.880
it takes to develop good products, is&nbsp;
to actually interact with customers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:34.400
look at the product and give you feedback on&nbsp;
either a demo or by actually using the product.&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:44.800
The argument was we should rapidly expand into&nbsp;
the different parts of the ML pipeline in parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.880
and leverage those connections and the&nbsp;
ability to raise money. We could build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.720
a team that could build products in each&nbsp;
of these spaces. I would say we didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:57.600
quite do that. I think we still have this&nbsp;
goal of expanding across the ML pipeline.&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:04.080
This early theory that maybe we're not better,&nbsp;
we may not be great at building products...I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.760
would say this is maybe not so humble of a&nbsp;
statement, but I think we built something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.720
users really love and we definitely did it by&nbsp;
hiring great engineers and great product people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.640
and by talking to these customers a ton&nbsp;
and spending lots and lots of time and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:22.880
having this relentless customer focus.
But I also think that somehow at core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.640
there's this magic somewhere in what we're&nbsp;
doing at W&amp;B and that we do understand the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:34.160
and the customers and we turn that money and&nbsp;
customer connection into great products. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.560
back then, I was thinking, "Well, we have great&nbsp;
products," and looking back now, I feel like we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.360
really do. That's really been a cool journey.
Lukas: All right. I have a question for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:49.680
both of you I was wondering about. Was there&nbsp;
a moment where you felt like the business was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.000
really working or the company was really working?&nbsp;
Can you think of a time when you really suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:02.160
felt like that or not? If so, what time was it?
Chris: I think there's a couple times, but one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:10.240
that really stands out for me is driving down to&nbsp;
Palo Alto or Mountain View or wherever we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.560
meeting, and one of our first deals&nbsp;
was with Toyota Research Institute.&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:19.440
I remember sitting out. I had&nbsp;
grabbed lunch with Ari, our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:25.600
first Account Executive. I grabbed lunch. We knew&nbsp;
we were going to go into this meeting and present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.920
the number that we were going to sell&nbsp;
our software for. I had this thought of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:40.240
"This moment is really important."
It's scary. When you're brand new, you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.000
many other customers and you go and you say, "Hey,&nbsp;
we want to charge this much for our software,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:52.560
I felt like this is make-or-break. We went in. The&nbsp;
meeting went really well and we ended up closing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:56.400
TRI is one of our first customers,&nbsp;
which is great. But after that, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:03.200
I wasn't like, "Okay, now we're set. Next week,&nbsp;
we can get to a billion dollar valuation," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:11.120
that first customer was really big.
Shawn: Yeah, that was a big one. As a founder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:17.360
or probably anybody at an early stage startup,&nbsp;
you say, "Oh, if we can just do this one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:23.040
then we'll be sure that we made it." Then the next&nbsp;
week, you're back to work, and you have to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:30.640
some more, and you're always looking at the next&nbsp;
thing. But that first customer was a great one.&nbsp;

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:36.640
I think for me, what comes to mind is — I think&nbsp;
this was maybe around the holidays two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:44.160
ago — in the earlier stages, even up to, say,&nbsp;
15, 20 people in the company, you kind of have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:49.360
pretty good sense of everything that's happening.
Every deal, I remember being a part of in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:54.080
form. But there was a moment around the holidays&nbsp;
a couple years ago where we have these Friday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.640
meetings where we get together and talk about&nbsp;
how the week went. It's everybody at the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:03.120
and somebody says something great that they did.
In that particular meeting, I just remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:08.240
there was somebody on the sales team who had made&nbsp;
another sale to a customer I had never talked to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:12.400
Somebody in the growth team that had found&nbsp;
a new growth experiment to do and executed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:17.040
it and actually made numbers change. Somebody&nbsp;
in the product team that had done something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.880
that I didn't even know they were doing.
All of those things came together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.240
in one meeting for me. That's when&nbsp;
I felt like, "Wow, this company is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:30.720
a lot more than...I can't wrap my arms around it&nbsp;
and push everything forward anymore. There is all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:39.280
these great people around me that are doing that."
That is an amazing feeling because from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:44.320
you add more and more great people and the&nbsp;
company continues to go in a good direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.520
It's bigger than yourself.
Lukas:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:53.120
Another question I had for both&nbsp;
of you is, is there a favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:59.360
feature in the product that you feel really proud&nbsp;
of or some way that the product works that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:03.920
feel like is uniquely great?
Chris: For me, it's got to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:08.000
command line interface. I think it's a very&nbsp;
under-appreciated interface to our product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:15.760
In the early days, I spent an obsessive amount&nbsp;
of time on making a whole bunch of command line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:20.480
commands and making it work nicely in&nbsp;
Unix. I was piping stuff at one point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:26.400
I think we've since decided that's not the way&nbsp;
most people want to interact with our product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.720
but as a Unix nerd, that's&nbsp;
my favorite part for sure.&nbsp;

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:41.120
Shawn: Maybe something that people&nbsp;
don't really see is, there is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:47.040
layer in the frontend that we made. In W&amp;B, you've&nbsp;
got all these different charts on the screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.840
The architecture of the frontend is that each&nbsp;
chart can individually make its own network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:56.240
request to get the data that it needs to show.
Those are actually heavy requests because there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:00.320
millions of data points sometimes if you've&nbsp;
logged a lot of data. So we built this cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:04.720
layer in the frontend, it's like a middleware&nbsp;
that watches all of the requests going out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.280
It aggregates them all together so it has a little&nbsp;
time delay. It says, "Give me all the requests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:14.720
that happened in the last hundred milliseconds."
It does lots of cool handwritten optimizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:19.360
to figure out how to merge certain kinds of&nbsp;
queries together and get all the results at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:23.280
once in a single request and then give that&nbsp;
back to the user. A lot of users, you have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:29.280
idea that's going on, but when you're building&nbsp;
UIs, you really want it to be snappy and fast.&nbsp;

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.040
I hope that I'm not shooting myself in the&nbsp;
foot. I'm sure somebody will have a story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:37.280
Weights &amp; Biases not being snappy and fast. If&nbsp;
you do, send it my way and we'll get it fixed.&nbsp;

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.720
But there is this massive amount of engineering&nbsp;
effort that went into that chunk of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:45.600
to make sure that charts with&nbsp;
millions of data points all on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:51.280
the screen at once can be updated really quickly.
Lukas: Just so people don't think I'm only asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:55.680
softball questions here, this is something that a&nbsp;
couple of candidates have asked me about recently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.680 --> 00:17:02.640
Have there been product or engineering efforts&nbsp;
that we would do a lot differently in hindsight?&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.960
Chris: I mean, I've got one that&nbsp;
isn't bad, so it's not fair.&nbsp;

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:13.440
When we first started the company, I wrote the&nbsp;
backend in Python. It was Python 2.0 because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:19.120
I wanted to use Google App Engine to start things&nbsp;
up so I didn't have to do a bunch of DevOps stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.840
That quickly stopped scaling, especially&nbsp;
because we have a GraphQL backend where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:28.880
things need to happen in parallel.
Our first engineer Tom actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:36.000
rewrote that entire backend in GoLang. When you&nbsp;
do a big rewrite like that...we already had TRI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:41.120
as a customer, OpenAI was a heavy user. We had&nbsp;
to keep the site up while this was happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:46.960
Usually those kinds of exercises can&nbsp;
go sideways. You can start to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:51.040
way longer than you would have anticipated or&nbsp;
the ultimate project wouldn't have worked out.&nbsp;

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:54.560
That was an example where Tom actually&nbsp;
got the project done ahead of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.200 --> 00:18:01.200
and we're still running pretty much the&nbsp;
same backend code to scale up to the tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:08.320
thousands of users that we have every day now.
It's not a decision I would go back and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:14.000
"Not do." Now that I had a good experience doing&nbsp;
a rewrite, I certainly wouldn't say we always do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:19.760
a rewrite, but you don't hear it often, our choice&nbsp;
to do the rewrite was definitely the right choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.480
and it worked out really well.
Lukas: What customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:28.480
feedback has surprised you the most?
Shawn: I have an example from early on when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:35.680
started the company, which was...the very first&nbsp;
version of Weights &amp; Biases was more of a command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:42.240
line tool around saving data. There was a little&nbsp;
bit of UI. Chris had built this Python library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:49.280
and this UI. The UI was essentially, what it did&nbsp;
was it let you log in and set up a place to store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:53.040
the data that the command line tool was saving.&nbsp;
But it didn't do a whole lot more than that.&nbsp;

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:58.800
I remember Chris added a feature that would&nbsp;
just kind of...it also collected the outputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:05.360
of your training runs, like the logs. Chris added&nbsp;
a feature that would look for the specific Keras&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.840
metrics that it printed over time and&nbsp;
he just made a line chart of that.&nbsp;

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:14.080
Of course, early on in the startup, what&nbsp;
you do is you have a demo or a thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:17.840
works you go to customers and you show it&nbsp;
to them. All the customers were like, "Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:23.120
command line tool. Okay, okay. But what's that&nbsp;
chart?" They would really focus on that chart.&nbsp;

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:27.040
The reason it was surprising to me is because&nbsp;
these are programmers and data scientists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:33.840
people who are really comfortable in Matplotlib&nbsp;
and Jupyter notebooks. A thesis you might have is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:38.560
well, data scientists don't need a tool to create&nbsp;
a bunch of charts in their browser because their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:42.320
use cases are going to be so different and&nbsp;
they're just comfortable doing it themselves.&nbsp;

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:46.320
It was really a big surprise to me that&nbsp;
that was the main thing people focused on.&nbsp;

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:52.240
We saw that and we said, "Well, let's follow what&nbsp;
users want," and we did that. We kept building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:57.760
the UI and making it better and better, and we&nbsp;
were able now to have a generic UI that solves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:00.960
lots of different kinds of use cases.&nbsp;
But it was surprising that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.480
would be possible for this kind of user.
Lukas: That's a good founding story right there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:11.440
Chris, what about you?
Chris: The most notable user feedback that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:19.280
top of mind is an early user, Hamel at GitHub, was&nbsp;
a heavy user of the tool. I remember one night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:25.280
Hamel wrote in and said, "Hey, we really want&nbsp;
to log HTML," and we were actually able to ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:33.680
that feature in that same night,&nbsp;
which was delighting to Hamel.&nbsp;

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:39.440
But Hamel was also...he did not hold&nbsp;
back in telling us where we were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:46.640
being excellent in the UI and was very&nbsp;
honest about some pretty serious issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:50.880
with the system at the time. I remember it&nbsp;
breaking my heart as a founder. Like here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:57.040
I've got someone who is engaged and excited about&nbsp;
us, but he's getting frustrated by using our tool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:06.640
It's the worst possible thing I could imagine.
The team really focused and did a lot of hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:14.320
work to redesign and re-engineer a lot of the&nbsp;
problem interfaces that Hamel was running into and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:17.040
ultimately I think it really&nbsp;
helped us make a better product.&nbsp;

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:23.840
Lukas: How do you think the last four and a&nbsp;
half years of running this kind of hyper-growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:27.360
startup has changed you as a person or&nbsp;
changed your perspective on the world?&nbsp;

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:32.240
Chris: I remember when we first started the&nbsp;
company, after Luke and I had been working at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:39.600
CrowdFlower for 10-plus years. I was just so&nbsp;
excited to have a blank canvas. It was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:47.200
"We can start fresh." There is nothing legacy&nbsp;
we need to support. It's just green fields.&nbsp;

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:55.440
I tend to think of the company stuff,&nbsp;
the process and the management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:01.840
and all of the things that you need to do to&nbsp;
make a company work, historically that didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:08.160
interest me that much. I think something has&nbsp;
changed, especially with this company. Now I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:14.560
find those things more interesting.
Being able to step away from just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:20.960
hacking all the time and actually think about,&nbsp;
"Okay, how do we build a culture and how do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:28.640
mentor and work with the team to ultimately&nbsp;
build a better product?" I think those problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:34.240
are much more interesting for me this time around&nbsp;
than they were when we were running CrowdFlower.&nbsp;

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:40.480
Lukas: Another question I had is, what do you&nbsp;
think has changed around us as we've been running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:45.200
this company? Do you feel like&nbsp;
customers are different now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:53.760
Do you feel like the industry is different at all?
Shawn: We started the company a year or two within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.760 --> 00:23:00.640
when deep learning was first...when AlexNet was&nbsp;
first trained or when AlexNet actually showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:06.640
real results. We really focused on deep learning.&nbsp;
I mean, one of our first customers was OpenAI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:11.280
That's well-known. They're still a great customer&nbsp;
of ours and we spend a lot of time building things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:19.440
that were tailored to OpenAI use cases.
When you start a company, it's good to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.840
make a bet on what you think a growing market&nbsp;
will be because you don't want to go into...you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:27.280
can do this, but you don't necessarily&nbsp;
want to go into a big established market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:32.240
and just fight with Google and Amazon. It's better&nbsp;
to focus maybe on something that's smaller and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.320
they won't spend all their resources fighting&nbsp;
you on, and then the market grows to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:40.720
along with you where all of a sudden,&nbsp;
you're this billion-dollar company.&nbsp;

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:47.440
You can't just do that. There's some amount&nbsp;
of luck, for sure. We had very good timing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.920
starting Weights &amp; Biases. That's a really cool&nbsp;
feeling and it's really cool to ride that trend.&nbsp;

00:23:54.800 --> 00:24:00.480
In doing that, what we've seen is deep learning&nbsp;
really took off. I mean, it's applied in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:06.720
vertical now. Every company has at least a few&nbsp;
people who are building deep learning models now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:10.240
Those teams are constantly growing&nbsp;
and we see that in the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:16.560
our contracts grow with our customers.
We sort of bet that that would happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.800
but to see it actually happen and to be able to&nbsp;
ride that trend, there is no way to really feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.560
what exponential growth is until you're in the&nbsp;
middle of it, and that's what it feels like.&nbsp;

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:33.760
Chris: I remember one moment early in the company&nbsp;
that stands out. We had presented to OpenAI, to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:39.680
group of researchers. I remember I was presenting&nbsp;
and half the audience was just looking at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:49.760
laptops. I left that meeting feeling like no one&nbsp;
cares. Then a week later, Woj calls us up and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:56.400
says, "Hey, we got this problem on the robotics&nbsp;
team. Come check this out. Can you help us out?"&nbsp;

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:00.480
We go, look, and I remember going there&nbsp;
with Shawn. Both Shawn and I are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:05.760
at it and we're excited, right? Because we&nbsp;
can solve the problem. They're telling us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:12.320
they have a problem and they want us to fix it.
After that, Shawn and I, we pulled an all-nighter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:18.880
just cranking out the interface that they&nbsp;
wanted and got it to them within a couple days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:23.680
I remember thinking, "How precious&nbsp;
is this relationship with OpenAI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:29.440
this institution that I really, really admire?"
Also, that same feeling that Shawn described of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:34.000
some users saying, "Hey, I have this&nbsp;
problem," and we had the power to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:37.520
and actually fix that problem for them.
Lukas: Do you remember the afternoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:41.840
when they turned on Weights &amp; Biases?
Shawn: That was another all-nighter.&nbsp;

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:45.040
Chris: Yeah, it turns out there was&nbsp;
some performance problems with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.920
Python backend if I'm recalling correctly.
Shawn: Well, there were a couple things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:54.240
We did not anticipate OpenAI's scale, because&nbsp;
we're doing the thing that you do as a startup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:59.760
which is you make an MVP. It doesn't really need&nbsp;
to scale. But it turns out our very first customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.320
was one of the largest-scale&nbsp;
customers we could have.&nbsp;

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:11.840
They were the first person who integrated Weights&nbsp;
&amp; Biases into this library that they had that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.520
everybody on their robotics team was using&nbsp;
to run training code. As soon as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:20.320
committed that to production, they started&nbsp;
sending us a ton of traffic. The site just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:23.840
immediately went down because it's&nbsp;
this cobbled together startup website.&nbsp;

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:29.440
Actually, the first problem was there&nbsp;
was some API limit that we hit on Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:33.760
because of the way we were making a specific&nbsp;
request. Chris might remember what it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:39.040
There was no resolution. You can't just call&nbsp;
Google and get them to immediately change a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:43.200
limit for you. You actually have to wait for a&nbsp;
support case to go through for a number of hours.&nbsp;

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:48.160
Of course, now, maybe we're a big enough customer&nbsp;
of Google that we could have some influence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.360
but back then, we were just this little startup&nbsp;
and we can't go, "But it's our first customer."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:57.520
That doesn't really sway anyone over there.
I don't remember how we worked around on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:02.480
Do you remember? Did we just wait?
Chris: We had very smartly designed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:11.280
Python library to back off when things&nbsp;
started failing, so I think the quota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:18.560
resolution got resolved within the retry timeout.
Shawn: That was one problem. Then later in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:22.000
evening, of course, we're pumped because we&nbsp;
have all this data coming, and it's OpenAI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:26.240
and it's our first customer.
Some other problem cropped up.&nbsp;

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:29.680
Again, you might remember this, but&nbsp;
it was something where Chris and I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:35.520
we were up until 5:00 AM that night and Chris was&nbsp;
live patching our app engine code. It was Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:40.800
App Engine at the time, which is this Python&nbsp;
auto-scaling platform that's not used as much now.&nbsp;

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:45.280
I remember, yeah, we came up with a plan&nbsp;
and we live patched the thing. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:49.840
"Is this going to work?" It did and the traffic&nbsp;
started coming in clean and we could see all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:55.040
data. We were so proud to have our first customer.
Then, of course, the next morning, we went to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:58.080
to OpenAI and they didn't even notice&nbsp;
the hiccup. They were like, "Oh, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:03.760
cool. Thanks." But, I mean, really, it was working&nbsp;
when we went to have that conversation and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:07.680
started looking at the sort of charts that&nbsp;
we had and started giving us that feedback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:12.480
That's when we got into the feedback cycle.
It's important. In the early days, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:18.000
have any customer at all and they have a problem,&nbsp;
stay up all night and solve their problem. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.280
if they don't notice it, it's worth it for you to&nbsp;
start getting that great customer feedback loop.&nbsp;

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:26.560
Lukas: What were your darkest&nbsp;
moments, specifically?&nbsp;

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:34.080
Chris: Early on when it was me&nbsp;
and Ari, that was the sales team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.280 --> 00:28:39.360
This was before the pandemic, so we would fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:48.720
wherever we needed to fly to. Some might&nbsp;
think, "Oh, you get to fly to Toronto?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:54.640
That's got to be great." It's not great.
You fly and then you go to some hotel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.640 --> 00:29:00.000
then you go to a meeting where you're just&nbsp;
trying to get people to engage with you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.360
and learn about the product. There were a couple&nbsp;
months there very early on where I felt like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:11.920
"We can't charge enough for the product,&nbsp;
people don't see it as being valuable enough."&nbsp;

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:18.080
It can be very demoralizing, especially when&nbsp;
you're out there on the front lines of sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:26.800
and trying to educate and teach people about these&nbsp;
concepts that are literally being created as we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.040
iterating on the product.
Shawn: I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:33.600
a dark moment for me is — we&nbsp;
already talked about this — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:39.120
when we were first trying to sell to GitHub,&nbsp;
we had this user, Hamel, who we talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:45.760
earlier. He gave us very direct feedback about&nbsp;
how our product sucked. He was totally right.&nbsp;

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:54.160
I love building this product and even now, it's&nbsp;
hard. I'll take it personally for sure. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:58.320
though I know that some of the decisions are bad&nbsp;
or there's lots of things that could be improved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:02.240
When somebody calls it out, it definitely&nbsp;
hurts. But we really want that feedback.&nbsp;

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:08.640
I'm happy to go through that rollercoaster&nbsp;
of emotions to make a better product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:13.280
Really, that feedback led Weights&nbsp;
&amp; Biases to the place it is today.&nbsp;

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:20.640
You have to be willing to accept there is lots of&nbsp;
bad things. We want to know what they are. We made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:25.440
those decisions. It's my fault. It's Chris' fault.&nbsp;
It's probably Lukas' fault and Lavanya's fault to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:33.120
some extent. We can always improve stuff.
You take those gut punches in stride and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:35.520
keep making it better.
Lukas: I have this memory — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:41.440
wonder if this is an accurate memory or you see&nbsp;
it the same way — but I remember thinking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:47.360
making experiment tracking and doing an offsite&nbsp;
where we really built something super custom for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:55.680
TRI, and then going there and showing them a beta&nbsp;
of the experiment tracking stuff that we built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.680 --> 00:31:00.720
and having them basically tell us this&nbsp;
isn't that interesting and that feeling bad.&nbsp;

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:05.120
Then I have this memory of talking to you, Shawn,&nbsp;
and I think you were like, "I don't think anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:08.960
will pay for experiment tracking." I was kind&nbsp;
of thinking, "Yeah, you're probably right."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:12.800
Then I remember talking to you, I was like,&nbsp;
"I just need to tell you that I need you to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:17.200
more positive," which is so funny because I feel&nbsp;
like actually you're almost always the optimist.&nbsp;

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:21.760
I remember at least thinking to myself, "What&nbsp;
I need to communicate is I just need you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:27.360
be positive even if it's not rational to be&nbsp;
positive here because I'm feeling a lot of doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:30.400
myself." Is that an accurate memory?
Shawn: Yeah, yeah. I remember. I remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:35.360
that. That was before we had that user, that&nbsp;
first user who was actually using the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:39.280
and we felt like they were getting value.
We kept saying, "What if we build this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.000
other thing? Will that get us a user? What&nbsp;
if we build this other thing?" We did that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:49.280
for a number of months. It was the early stages&nbsp;
of the startup. That was disheartening because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:53.520
it's like, okay, if we could build this other&nbsp;
feature tomorrow, but nobody's going to care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:57.680
That was kind of the mindset I was getting&nbsp;
into and you were rightfully calling me out on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:02.800
well, this is the early stage of a startup, so&nbsp;
let's make the next thing until we have that user.&nbsp;

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:10.000
It was getting that first user who&nbsp;
broke that off. From there, it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:16.240
"Hey, I just need this one little tweak. Great,&nbsp;
we'll do it," and it was all positive. Maybe not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:21.600
all positive, but more positive from there.
Chris: All right, Luke. If there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:26.800
other entrepreneurs listening to the podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:37.680
and wanting to build a startup that achieves&nbsp;
a billion-dollar valuation, what advice as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:41.680
startup CEO would you give them?
Lukas: I feel like the advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:46.560
probably depends on who that person is. Let's&nbsp;
picture someone. Who are you thinking of here?&nbsp;

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:51.520
Chris: All right. It's someone that looks a&nbsp;
little like us, right? They're programmers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:58.080
They're interested in starting a company,&nbsp;
but maybe don't have a ton of experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:01.760
on the business side of things, but they're&nbsp;
passionate about the product they're creating.&nbsp;

00:33:03.013 --> 00:33:07.200
Lukas: There's so much advice out there that I&nbsp;
think is really good these days. I feel like when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:12.400
we were all starting our companies the first&nbsp;
time, being an entrepreneur wasn't a thing.&nbsp;

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:17.440
Y Combinator's put out so much good&nbsp;
stuff that's really...you forget how not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:21.200
obvious it is to people that you need to make&nbsp;
something that people want, right? You can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:26.560
emphasize that enough, right?
I feel like now people kind of know that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:32.000
which is fantastic. It definitely wasn't obvious&nbsp;
to everyone when we were starting or maybe as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:36.640
obvious to us as it should have been.
I think the thing that people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:41.680
talk about as much as I think they should or the&nbsp;
advice that I feel like I can uniquely offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:49.200
because it's worked so well for me, is to pick a&nbsp;
customer that you really love spending time with.&nbsp;

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:56.480
I feel like a lot of these ML startups especially,&nbsp;
they totally start from a technology and what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.480 --> 00:34:02.080
interesting to do with it. That's a bad idea.&nbsp;
Everyone kind of knows that's a bad idea. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:09.520
they work backwards from a use case that they find&nbsp;
interesting and that's maybe...it's an okay idea.&nbsp;

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:15.840
The thing that gets lost is that, at&nbsp;
least for me, the thing I do as CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:21.040
the thing I have to do all day long is spending&nbsp;
time with customers, spending time empathizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:26.080
with customers, thinking about customers and&nbsp;
bringing the customer voice into the company.&nbsp;

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:34.400
Given that that's, I think, maybe the most&nbsp;
important job as CEO, you should pick a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:37.680
customer that you really like, right? Because&nbsp;
you're going to spend so much time with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:45.600
over the entire arc of your company. Having a&nbsp;
specific idea of who that is and making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.600 --> 00:34:51.680
you like them, I think, is a really key thing.
I remember at CrowdFlower, we tried to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:57.520
into different types of customers and so I&nbsp;
really felt this. I went to CMO conferences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:05.440
I contrast that for myself going to NeurIPS and&nbsp;
just really enjoying making small talk, enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:10.480
all the details, the things that people say.
I also believe it's very powerful for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:14.160
world and good for the world, but I think&nbsp;
even more than that, just on a day-to-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:19.920
motivation...the impact will sustain you&nbsp;
over the long-term, but over the short-term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:24.960
I think I really appreciate that&nbsp;
I'm working with a user base that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:30.720
really care about and enjoy talking to.
Shawn: Lavanya's in the chat here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:35.360
We're taking questions from the audience.
Lukas: What's the hope for Weights &amp; Biases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:41.680
in the next five years? I feel like&nbsp;
almost it's like a jinx to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:44.880
question. I don't even know if I have a good&nbsp;
answer. Maybe if you guys want to try first?&nbsp;

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:50.720
Shawn: Of course, I take it from a product&nbsp;
and tools standpoint. I hope that we can build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:57.760
interconnected tools across the ML pipeline&nbsp;
that really work well together because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.760 --> 00:36:04.080
share these common underlying threads or&nbsp;
infrastructural pieces or, dare I say, bones?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:08.560
Which is what I like to call them internally&nbsp;
and everybody makes fun of me for.&nbsp;

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.320
To me, it's really important that&nbsp;
the data that you collect about your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:17.920
model in production can be used to inform&nbsp;
decisions that you make back in the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:21.600
collection process and the training process.
There's so many parts of the ML pipeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:26.000
it's hard to build all this stuff. But&nbsp;
if you think of the best companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.720 --> 00:36:30.480
somebody like Google who's building ML, they've&nbsp;
built all this. They've verticalized all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:34.400
it internally and built it themselves.&nbsp;
They've built tools out of other tools.&nbsp;

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:40.720
I want to be able to make a platform like&nbsp;
that outside of a giant company and give it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:44.640
to the rest of the world and use all the&nbsp;
use cases that we encountered to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:48.880
it better and better, and more general.
Lukas: I think it'd be really satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.040 --> 00:36:58.080
if Weights &amp; Biases becomes a core part of&nbsp;
every ML team's infrastructure and we're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:04.640
known for making really high quality stuff,&nbsp;
really useful stuff, really powerful stuff.&nbsp;

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:10.240
I think we're on that trajectory, but I&nbsp;
think ML is growing so much that that becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:16.240
every company when every company has an ML team,&nbsp;
which it seems like we're headed to in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:18.880
five years. So I think that's the biggest thing.&nbsp;

00:37:20.613 --> 00:37:26.880
Shawn: If you imagine the company that is in&nbsp;
that position, what does it look like internally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:29.840
I think we have this today.
But, I mean, there's folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:34.400
building data tools and ML tools at lots of&nbsp;
companies in the world and doing a great job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:39.920
I want to get all those people in the same&nbsp;
place. People who love building these tools.&nbsp;

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:44.400
Maybe there's folks out there who work on these&nbsp;
tools and don't really love it and want to switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:48.480
to something else. That's great too. I mean, you&nbsp;
should move through things in your career. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:52.560
I would love to be surrounded by people who&nbsp;
really love this problem and really love the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:56.720
people who work on this problem, these&nbsp;
customers, just all together, all of us.&nbsp;

00:37:57.360 --> 00:38:01.440
Maybe not in an office in the modern&nbsp;
world anymore, but distributed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:05.840
working on these problems together and just&nbsp;
building great stuff that users love so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:11.680
can build ML models that make the world better.
Lukas: Here on Gradient Dissent, since you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:19.120
are both mega fans, you know this, but for new&nbsp;
listeners, we always end with two questions. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:26.800
penultimate question is what is the most,&nbsp;
or an underrated topic in machine learning?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:32.160
Something that you would love to work on&nbsp;
if you weren't working on Weights &amp; Biases.&nbsp;

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:41.760
Chris: l want to make a painting robot. But&nbsp;
it uses an actual brush, okay? Not just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:49.200
plotter or something. It's going to be very&nbsp;
complicated. That's not the big world-changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:54.720
answer maybe you were looking for, but&nbsp;
that's what I would be really stoked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:58.320
pour a year of my life into I think.
Lukas: Like a paintbot?&nbsp;

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:01.200
Chris: Yeah, a paintbot. That's right.
Lukas: Cool, cool.&nbsp;

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:07.360
Shawn: Underrated. Underrated topic. Maybe&nbsp;
we should have started that robot company...&nbsp;

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:14.000
This question's funny because we touch so&nbsp;
many of the problems in machine learning at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:18.640
Weights &amp; Biases. Not all of them, but we're&nbsp;
building tools that are used for all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:23.120
verticals, so of course we're going to touch them.
So maybe I'll say something that we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.120 --> 00:39:27.120
explicitly doing, that I think is&nbsp;
really important, and I feel like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:32.720
am actually doing, which is...I think model&nbsp;
understanding is critical in the future.&nbsp;

00:39:33.600 --> 00:39:37.120
Deep learning models are really tricky&nbsp;
to understand. It's a research area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:41.840
It's really dependent on what kind of model&nbsp;
you're building, what techniques you might use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:47.440
to figure out "Why did my car make the decision&nbsp;
that it did at a specific point in time?"&nbsp;

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:54.880
I think as these models get more and more complex,&nbsp;
it's more and more important. I want to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:58.480
the models. I want to understand the models for&nbsp;
the world to be good and I want to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:58.480 --> 00:40:04.400
the models because I think that gives us some&nbsp;
understanding into the nature of intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:10.880
and our own decision-making processes.
We're not explicitly doing model understanding at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.880 --> 00:40:16.320
Weights &amp; Biases, but we're trying to build tools,&nbsp;
and we'll talk more about this over the next six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:21.760
months, I guess, that head in that direction.
Lukas: I'm tempted to answer this question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:25.440
but I feel like maybe it's better as a host&nbsp;
if I remain mysterious in terms of what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:31.200
think is the most underrated topic.
I will say I love the company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:36.480
we started and I would not change it. Obviously&nbsp;
it was a really good choice to do it. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:40.720
thing that we kicked around in the early&nbsp;
days... Well, we kicked around two ideas.&nbsp;

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:43.360
One I think is a terrible idea,&nbsp;
which is a painting drone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:49.440
which I think would be fun, but probably just&nbsp;
terrible idea. But still, it would be really fun.&nbsp;

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:54.640
The second idea which I think I would still&nbsp;
love to do it, and I think we thought we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:58.480
were the wrong team to do it, but actually I&nbsp;
think we might have been a good team to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:02.960
but the timing seems like it might have been&nbsp;
bad, which is to build a better simulator to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:08.560
help robot companies simulate what they're&nbsp;
doing and then deploy into the real world.&nbsp;

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:12.880
I just think that that still kind of needs&nbsp;
to exist. There's different takes on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:16.960
but it doesn't seem like it's been nailed&nbsp;
at all, especially with the physical part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:24.640
it. I also think that would be a really fun&nbsp;
company to do, although a much slower ride.&nbsp;

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:28.960
But my answer to the most underrated&nbsp;
topic in ML is still a secret.&nbsp;

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:36.720
Lukas: The final question is when you&nbsp;
look at actually all of our customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:42.240
trying to deploy models successfully, what&nbsp;
do you think their biggest pain point is?&nbsp;

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:45.840
We always ask guests this who are mostly&nbsp;
at these companies trying to do it, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:50.800
looking out at everyone —&nbsp;
maybe we restrict this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:53.680
people already using Weights &amp; Biases&nbsp;
because to people not using somebody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:56.560
obviously maybe that's their biggest problem&nbsp;
— but the people that are already using us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.560 --> 00:42:02.000
what is the big thing that they run into?
Shawn: I mean, there is a cop-out answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:08.000
which is it's probably hiring.
Lukas: That might be accurate. Hiring? Yeah.&nbsp;

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:12.720
At some point, we should collect some stats&nbsp;
on what people say and maybe that will just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.040
answer it based on...at least, people we&nbsp;
interview. But yeah, what are you guys seeing?&nbsp;

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:22.560
Shawn: It really varies in our customers because&nbsp;
somebody who is building a self-driving car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:28.400
for example, is they're building 100 models in&nbsp;
parallel and with no proof that self-driving cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:32.320
could still actually exist and work on public&nbsp;
streets. I guess, we're getting very close now.&nbsp;

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:38.080
As opposed to somebody maybe who's got a&nbsp;
bunch of financial data and needs to predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:43.360
credit scores. In the credit score problem,&nbsp;
you actually...model understanding, what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:46.800
was just talking about, really, really important.&nbsp;
You probably need to use something dumber than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:51.760
a deep learning model so that you can actually&nbsp;
say why you made a particular credit prediction.&nbsp;

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:57.440
Our customers are extremely varied.&nbsp;
I hope we're solving a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.440 --> 00:43:01.840
problems in the model creation side of things.&nbsp;
I think that there is a really hard problem of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:05.920
figuring out what models are doing in production&nbsp;
and then taking the data from production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:09.520
and integrating it back into&nbsp;
the model training process.&nbsp;

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:15.760
I hope that we get to work on that problem too,&nbsp;
but I bet that a lot of our customers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:20.240
express that they have challenges there.
Chris: Yeah, I'll piggyback on Shawn's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.240 --> 00:43:30.320
response and say specifically CI and&nbsp;
CD, when it comes to these ML pipelines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:34.640
just is nowhere close to what we have in&nbsp;
the regular software development world.&nbsp;

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:42.640
I know we have a lot of exciting things on our&nbsp;
roadmap to help with automating all of these steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:48.640
as a model moves through the pipeline and then&nbsp;
comes back to get retrained and understanding how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:55.920
it's performing in production, but I personally&nbsp;
look forward to the day that all of this can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.720 --> 00:44:04.400
automated in a way that doesn't involve people&nbsp;
manually running shell scripts, which is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:09.680
the case today and really unfortunate.
Lukas: Awesome. Well, thanks so much, guys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:13.600
It's been a real pleasure working with&nbsp;
you and can't wait for many more years.&nbsp;

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:17.280
Shawn: You guys too.
Chris: Can't wait for more podcasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:19.120
Luke. You're a fantastic podcast host.
Lukas: Yeah, we got to bring you back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:24.560
a year from now, see where we're at.
Lukas: If you're enjoying these interviews and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:29.920
you want to learn more, please click on the link&nbsp;
to the show notes in the description where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.920 --> 00:44:35.680
find links to all the papers that are mentioned,&nbsp;
supplemental material, and a transcription that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:39.200
worked really hard to produce. So check it out.
Lukas: I thought it'd be fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:47.600
since it's a friendly guest obviously, to&nbsp;
add some zany new features to this podcast.&nbsp;

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:51.920
I thought one section would be..I'm going to&nbsp;
name a technology and then you got to immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:57.840
say underrated or overrated, and then if you&nbsp;
disagree, we can fight it out. What do you think?&nbsp;

00:44:57.840 --> 00:44:59.360
Shawn: Let's go.
Lukas: Are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:04.240
ready? Okay. Reinforcement learning.
Shawn: ls there a middle ground? Wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:07.280
is it only under or over?
Lukas: Yeah, you got to decide.&nbsp;

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:13.200
Shawn: I love reinforcement learning. Underrated.
Chris: Yeah, I'll go with Shawn, underrated.&nbsp;

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:17.840
Lukas: All right, all right. AutoML.
Chris: Overrated.&nbsp;

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:22.800
Shawn: Yeah.
Chris: Come on, Shawn. It's bad for business.&nbsp;

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:27.200
Shawn: Underrated.
Lukas:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:29.520
I already forgot what you said,&nbsp;
Chris. You think it's overrated?&nbsp;

00:45:29.520 --> 00:45:32.480
Chris: Yeah, yeah.
Lukas: Wait, why?&nbsp;

00:45:32.480 --> 00:45:36.880
Chris: Because I think it's really&nbsp;
important that the ML practitioner is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:37.440 --> 00:45:42.560
using their own creative powers to make&nbsp;
choices about how the model is architected.&nbsp;

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:48.560
It's like automation everywhere&nbsp;
else. I have mixed feelings about it.&nbsp;

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:53.840
Lukas: Shawn, you want a quick rebuttal to that?
Shawn: Yeah, I mean, I'm torn on this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:57.120
There is two good answers,&nbsp;
but I do think...of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:01.680
the technology to train models should&nbsp;
evolve over time and things should get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:07.200
smarter. For example, our Sweeps tool does&nbsp;
help you find good parameters for a model.&nbsp;

00:46:08.720 --> 00:46:14.080
I think as a company, what we need to do&nbsp;
is as those tools get better and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.080 --> 00:46:17.520
at automatically building models, there's&nbsp;
all kinds of other problems around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:22.000
model building, like getting the right data in&nbsp;
the first place, and we need to build those.&nbsp;

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:27.840
This space is moving so quickly, and of course&nbsp;
AutoML will probably continue to improve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.640 --> 00:46:32.320
but there's lots of other problems around&nbsp;
it to be solved for the practitioners.&nbsp;

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:36.400
Lukas: All right. Well, I think people&nbsp;
should leave comments for which they found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:40.000
more convincing, but definitely bonus points for&nbsp;
the Weights &amp; Biases plug in there. I like it.&nbsp;

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:45.120
Okay, next one. The singularity.
Chris: Underrated.&nbsp;

00:46:45.120 --> 00:46:51.200
Shawn: Yup. I'm with Chris. Underrated.
Lukas: Whoa, whoa. Bold. All right. We'll move on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:55.840
Okay, ready? Bigtable.
Chris: Overrated.&nbsp;

00:46:55.840 --> 00:47:00.400
Shawn: I'll go with underrated. Sure.
Lukas: Whoa, all right. Shawn, maybe you go first.&nbsp;

